      <img class="text-decoration-right"
        src="/assets/img/Smoky Mountain Acres/text-decoration-half-leaf.webp"
        alt="">
    </div>
    <p>Grazers that help us pay attention to forage, pasture, and the changing seasons.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="container">
    <div class="row gy-4 align-items-center">
      <div class="col-lg-5">
        <img src="/assets/img/Smoky Mountain Acres/Khatadin.webp"
          class="img-fluid"
          alt="Katahdin sheep standing on pasture at Smoky Mountain Acres">
      </div>
      <div class="col-lg-7 content">
        <h3>Katahdin Sheep</h3>
        <p>
          Our Katahdin sheep bring a hardy, traditional presence to the farm and help us make use of pasture in a way that is different from pigs or chickens. They graze grasses, clover, and other suitable forage as they move through the field.
        </p>
        <p>
          Pasture quality, weather, age, pregnancy, growth, and body condition all affect what sheep need. When pasture alone is not enough, we provide hay, minerals, or appropriate supplemental feed rather than forcing an unrealistic one-size-fits-all system.
        </p>
        <p>
          As the flock develops, we may offer lambs or breeding and meat stock. Current animals and availability will be shared through our livestock information when those offerings are ready.
        </p>
        <a href="/livestock/" class="offer-link">LEARN ABOUT FUTURE LIVESTOCK SALES</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

